# Patch 0084 — Focus mode (ROADMAP Phase 7; FEATURES 11 "block distracting
# sites + notifications").
#
# A SESSION IS A PERIOD, NOT A MODE FLAG. Everything reads one end time, and
# that single decision settles the awkward cases without extra machinery: a
# crash cannot strand the browser in a permanently blocked state (the session
# is over when the clock says so, whether or not a timer survived), and the
# request check never has to trust that some earlier cleanup ran. The end time
# is stored in SECONDS because prefs are 32-bit signed and a millisecond
# timestamp overflows into a negative number — which is precisely the shape of
# bug that ships as "focus mode randomly does nothing".
#
# SESSIONS SURVIVE A RESTART, deliberately. Quitting is the most obvious way
# to defeat a self-imposed block, and a focus feature a restart switches off is
# decoration. What it is NOT is a lock: ending early is one click, and the
# block page says so in as many words. The point is to make the distraction
# deliberate, not impossible.
#
# TOP-LEVEL PAGES ONLY, NOT SUBRESOURCES. Kavacha already ships tracking
# protection for the other job; quietly breaking embeds inside pages the user
# deliberately opened would make "focus mode" mean "the web is subtly broken
# now". Matching is on eTLD+1, so a rule for youtube.com covers m.youtube.com
# and does not cover notyoutube.com.
#
# THE NOTIFICATION DEFAULT IS RESTORED EXACTLY, and this is patch 0066's
# lesson applied BEFORE the bug rather than after. That patch armed a shared
# shutdown pref, could not tell "I set this" from "the user set this", and
# wiped browsing history on every quit for two weeks. Here the pre-session
# value is parked in its own pref (so it outlives the process, as the session
# does), and it is written back only if the current value is still the one we
# set — if it moved underneath us, the user's value wins and we drop our
# bookkeeping.
#
# CANCEL-THEN-REDIRECT IS DONE OFF THE NOTIFICATION STACK. The blocked channel
# is cancelled inside http-on-modify-request, but the block page is loaded
# from dispatchToMainThread: navigating a docshell from inside the observer
# re-enters the very machinery that is mid-flight.
#
# ONE PAGE IS BOTH THE WALL AND THE SETTINGS. about:focus leads with the block
# when it was reached by being blocked (?url=) and is the blocklist editor
# otherwise, because the moment someone most wants to change a rule is the
# moment they just hit it — and sending them elsewhere is how a rule that no
# longer makes sense survives for months. A blocked page whose session has
# since ended says that instead of showing a wall with nothing behind it.
#
# Per-Space lists are additive ("also block these"), not scoped ("only here"):
# asking which Space a request belongs to at http-on-modify-request time is
# neither cheap nor reliable, and a session is one span of attention anyway.
# Stated in the code as well, because the other reading is reasonable.
#
